course: dictionary
lesson: 9601
title: Strict: rules that must be obeyed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A strict person makes people follow rules and does not let bad behaviour go. A strict rule must be obeyed exactly. Strict can also mean exact and careful, as in «in strict order». Ask yourself: is the word about a person, a rule, or exactness? That tells you which meaning fits.
explain: «In Bradford, Tariq's dad is strict and religious and warm underneath, says no to a sleepover party, then drives Tariq there and picks him up at nine.» Strict describes a dad who sets firm limits, but the word warm tells you he is not cruel. Strict does not always mean unkind.
explain: «Good sports clubs have strict rules against fighting outside the club.» Here strict means the rules must be followed with no exceptions. Try swapping in firm: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
explain: In maths and science, strictly means exact. «Strictly greater than» means bigger, not equal. You may also hear «strictly speaking» when someone wants to be exact about the truth.
fact: «Strict» means making people obey rules and not allowing bad behaviour.
fact: A strict rule is one that must be obeyed exactly, with no exceptions.
fact: «Strict» can also mean exact and careful, as in «in strict order».
fact: «Strictly greater than» means bigger, not equal.
fact: Strict is not always the same as cruel: a strict person can still be warm and kind.
exercise: «Tariq's dad is strict, but he still drives Tariq to the sleepover.» What does strict describe in this sentence? | A dad who likes sleepovers | A dad who is always silent | *A dad who sets firm limits | A dad who never goes out
exercise: In maths, «strictly greater than» means what? | The same as | *Bigger, not equal | Roughly about | Smaller than
exercise: Find and copy the word in «"Eat your peas or you won't get pudding"» that sounds stricter. | won't | peas | *or | pudding
test: «Bailiffs can collect money owed only under strict rules, and cannot take essentials.» In this sentence, strict rules mean | *Rules that must be followed exactly | Friendly rules that are easy to bend | Rules about what to eat | Rules made up by the children
test: What does «a strict vegetarian» mean? | Someone who loves vegetables but eats meat | Someone who is very loud at dinner | Someone who eats fish every day | *Someone who follows the rule of eating no meat, exactly
exam: Ella says strict always means cross and angry. What is the best answer? | *No, strict can mean firm rules that must be obeyed, or exact and careful | No, strict means fast and quick | Yes, but only in maths | Yes, strict always means cross and angry
